[~accountid:616fd1373e3753006f97f319] Initial observations:

# AI generated images are not protected by copyright (copyright protects works made by humans), meaning that if we use such images and somebody copies us, there is no copyright infringement and we cannot request the “infringer” to cease the use of a copy;
# Images made by humans that incorporate AI-produced images can be protected;
# AI models could potentially create new images that “mirror” an original work (imitation) or the style of the author of the original work (derivative work), leading to risks of copyright infringement.
# A [class-action lawsuit for copyright infringement against|https://stablediffusionlitigation.com/pdf/00201/1-1-stable-diffusion-complaint.pdf] Midjourney and other AI image creating companies has been filed in the US. According to the plaintiffs the use of their copyright protected images in the training of Stable Diffusion (AI Image product that Midjourney uses to train its program and create images) amounts to copyright infringement. In addition, the plaintiffs claim that AI images can be created in the "style of" a specific author thereby profiting off the author’s distinct artistic identities which are uniquely associated with the author. 
# Midjourney [terms of service|https://docs.midjourney.com/docs/terms-of-service] need to be reviewed. I understand that Bolt “must purchase a “Pro” membership for every individual accessing the Services on Your behalf in order to own Assets You create”. Non-paid members receive a license for non-commercial use only.

